  <!-- G1 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g1">
    <div class="topic-header">
      <div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">01</div>
      <div class="topic-title-block">
        <div class="topic-label">Foundations</div>
        <div class="topic-title">Bioinformatics &amp; Its <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Role in Statistics</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">🧬</span><div class="card-lbl">What it is</div><h3>An Interdisciplinary Field</h3><p>Bioinformatics combines biology, computer science, mathematics, and statistics to collect, store, manage, analyse, and interpret biological data — DNA sequences, RNA transcripts, protein structures, gene-expression profiles. It develops algorithms and statistical methods to turn raw biological data into biological knowledge.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">⚙️</span><div class="card-lbl">Statistics' Contribution</div><h3>The Inferential Backbone</h3><ul><li><strong>Experimental design:</strong> replication, randomisation, blocking in microarray/RNA-seq studies</li><li><strong>Hypothesis testing:</strong> t-tests, ANOVA, LOD-score tests for linkage</li><li><strong>Multiple testing correction:</strong> FDR (Benjamini–Hochberg) across thousands of genes/markers</li><li><strong>Regression &amp; GLMs:</strong> QTL regression, RNA-seq count models, GWAS logistic regression</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">More Contributions</div><h3>Probability, ML &amp; Bayesian Methods</h3><ul><li><strong>Probability models:</strong> Markov chains/HMMs for sequence alignment; Poisson/negative-binomial for read counts</li><li><strong>Multivariate &amp; ML:</strong> clustering, PCA, classification for -omics data</li><li><strong>Bayesian inference:</strong> incorporating prior biological knowledge into gene mapping</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-warn"><span class="card-icon">⚠️</span><div class="card-lbl">Why It Matters</div><h3>Trust in the Signal</h3><p>Modern biology produces extremely large, high-dimensional, noisy datasets. Without statistics' inferential rigour (significance, confidence, error control), patterns found by bioinformatics tools could not be trusted as real rather than due to chance.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><strong>Memory Tip</strong>Bio + Informatics = using computational and statistical tools to make sense of biological information.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G2 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g2">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">02</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Cell Biology</div><div class="topic-title">Mitosis vs <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Meiosis</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">🔁</span><div class="card-lbl">Mitosis</div><h3>Growth, Repair &amp; Asexual Reproduction</h3><ul><li>One division (PMAT: Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase)</li><li>2 daughter cells, diploid (2n)</li><li>Genetically identical to the parent cell</li><li>No homologous pairing or crossing over</li><li>Occurs in somatic cells</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">🧬</span><div class="card-lbl">Meiosis</div><h3>Production of Gametes</h3><ul><li>Two successive divisions (Meiosis I &amp; II)</li><li>4 daughter cells, haploid (n)</li><li>Genetically unique — recombined</li><li>Homologous pairing (synapsis) in Prophase I</li><li>Crossing over present — generates recombinants</li><li>Occurs in germ cells (gonads)</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">Key Distinction</div><h3>Why Both Exist</h3><p>Mitosis maintains genetic constancy across somatic cell generations. Meiosis introduces genetic variation (via independent assortment and crossing over) while halving chromosome number, so fertilisation restores the diploid state in offspring.</p></div>
    </div>

