  <!-- G3 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g3">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">03</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Linkage</div><div class="topic-title">Genetic Linkage, Recombination Fraction &amp; <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Map Distance</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">🔗</span><div class="card-lbl">Genetic Linkage</div><h3>Inherited Together</h3><p>Two or more loci located close together on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together more often than expected under independent assortment — because physically close loci are less likely to be separated by a crossover. Linkage is an exception to Mendel's Law of Independent Assortment.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">📐</span><div class="card-lbl">Recombination Fraction (r)</div><h3>Proportion of Recombinants</h3><p>r = (number of recombinant offspring) / (total offspring). r = 0 → complete linkage; r = 0.5 → independent assortment (unlinked); 0 &lt; r &lt; 0.5 → partial linkage (smaller r = loci physically closer).</p></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">Map Distance</div><h3>Morgans &amp; centiMorgans</h3><p>Map distance expresses physical separation in terms of expected crossover frequency (Morgans/cM); 1 cM ≈ 1% recombination for closely-spaced loci. For distant loci, multiple crossovers can cancel out and go undetected, so observed r underestimates true distance — mapping functions correct for this.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="eq-block">
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Recombination fraction</span><span class="eq-val">r = (# recombinant offspring) / (total offspring)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Range &amp; meaning</span><span class="eq-val">r=0: complete linkage &nbsp;|&nbsp; r=0.5: unlinked &nbsp;|&nbsp; 0&lt;r&lt;0.5: partial linkage</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Map unit</span><span class="eq-val">1 cM ≈ 1% recombination (short distances only)</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G4 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g4">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">04</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Molecular Biology</div><div class="topic-title">Central Dogma &amp; <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Roles of RNA</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">🧪</span><div class="card-lbl">The Central Dogma</div><h3>Crick (1958)</h3><p>Describes the directional flow of genetic information: DNA → (transcription) → RNA → (translation) → Protein. DNA also replicates (DNA→DNA). In retroviruses, reverse transcription (RNA→DNA) is an exception to the classical dogma.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">⚙️</span><div class="card-lbl">5 Steps</div><h3>Replication to Maturation</h3><ul><li><strong>1. Replication:</strong> semi-conservative copying of DNA</li><li><strong>2. Transcription:</strong> RNA polymerase synthesises pre-mRNA from the DNA template (nucleus)</li><li><strong>3. RNA processing:</strong> 5' capping, splicing, 3' polyadenylation → mature mRNA</li><li><strong>4. Translation:</strong> ribosomes read codons; tRNA delivers amino acids; rRNA catalyses peptide bonds</li><li><strong>5. Post-translational modification:</strong> folding &amp; chemical modification into a mature protein</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">Roles of RNA</div><h3>Beyond mRNA</h3><ul><li><strong>mRNA:</strong> template for protein synthesis</li><li><strong>tRNA:</strong> delivers amino acids via anticodon–codon matching</li><li><strong>rRNA:</strong> structural/catalytic ribosome component</li><li><strong>miRNA / siRNA:</strong> post-transcriptional gene silencing</li><li><strong>snRNA:</strong> spliceosome component (intron removal)</li><li><strong>snoRNA:</strong> guides rRNA methylation</li><li><strong>lncRNA:</strong> regulates gene expression &amp; chromatin structure</li></ul></div>
    </div>

    <div class="viz-wrap">
      <div class="viz-title">Central Dogma — Information Flow</div>
