  <!-- G5 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g5">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">05</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Quantitative Genetics</div><div class="topic-title">Quantitative Trait Loci (QTL) &amp; <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Regression Models</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">📍</span><div class="card-lbl">What is a QTL?</div><h3>A Mapped Genomic Region</h3><p>A QTL is a genome region statistically associated with variation in a continuous trait (body weight, milk yield, blood pressure). Identified not by direct observation of a single gene but by testing association between marker genotypes and phenotype in a segregating population.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">⚖️</span><div class="card-lbl">QTL vs Mendelian Locus</div><h3>Key Differences</h3><ul><li><strong>Trait type:</strong> continuous (QTL) vs discrete (Mendelian)</li><li><strong>Genes:</strong> many, small effects (QTL) vs single, major effect (Mendelian)</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> regression/association (QTL) vs segregation ratios &amp; χ² (Mendelian)</li><li><strong>Penetrance:</strong> incomplete, polygenic background (QTL) vs complete (Mendelian)</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">Simple QTL Regression</div><h3>Marker-Based Mapping</h3><p>Phenotype regressed on genotype score at a marker: yᵢ = μ + b·xᵢ + eᵢ, eᵢ~N(0,σ²). If the marker is linked to a QTL, b differs significantly from zero.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-form"><span class="card-icon">🔢</span><div class="card-lbl">F2 Additive–Dominance Model</div><h3>Three Genotype Classes</h3><p>For AA / Aa / aa with additive score x = +1, 0, −1 and dominance score z = 0, 1, 0: yᵢ = μ + a·xᵢ + d·zᵢ + eᵢ. a = additive effect (half the homozygote-mean difference); d = dominance effect (heterozygote deviation from mid-parent).</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="eq-block">
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Simple QTL regression</span><span class="eq-val">yᵢ = μ + b·xᵢ + eᵢ &nbsp;&nbsp;, eᵢ ~ N(0,σ²)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">F2 additive–dominance model</span><span class="eq-val">yᵢ = μ + a·xᵢ + d·zᵢ + eᵢ</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Additive effect</span><span class="eq-val">â = (ȳ_AA − ȳ_aa) / 2</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Dominance effect</span><span class="eq-val">d̂ = ȳ_Aa − (ȳ_AA + ȳ_aa)/2</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Two-genotype t-test</span><span class="eq-val">t = (ȳ₁−ȳ₀) / √[sp²(1/n₁+1/n₀)] &nbsp;~ t(n₁+n₀−2)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Pooled variance</span><span class="eq-val">sp² = [(n₁−1)s₁² + (n₀−1)s₀²] / (n₁+n₀−2)</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><strong>One-Way ANOVA for 3 F2 Genotype Groups</strong>H₀: μ_AA=μ_Aa=μ_aa. SST=SSB+SSW; F = MSB/MSW ~ F(2, n−3). If F exceeds the critical value, the marker shows an overall genotype effect — evidence it is linked to a QTL. Follow-up t-tests on â and d̂ separately test the additive and dominance components.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G6 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g6">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">06</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Mapping Mechanics</div><div class="topic-title">Crossing Over &amp; Two-Point Linkage Analysis <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">(LOD Score)</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">✂️</span><div class="card-lbl">Crossing Over</div><h3>Physical Exchange of Segments</h3><p>Crossing over is the exchange of chromosomal segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during pachytene (Prophase I). A crossover between loci A and B produces recombinant chromatids (Ab, aB) differing from parental (AB, ab). P(crossover) — and hence r — increases with physical distance, the basis of genetic mapping.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">🧮</span><div class="card-lbl">F1 Gamete Classes</div><h3>Parental vs Recombinant</h3><p>From F1 = AB/ab (coupling phase): gamete AB (parental, freq (1−r)/2), ab (parental, (1−r)/2), Ab (recombinant, r/2), aB (recombinant, r/2). With a fully informative marker, every offspring gamete is unambiguously classified, giving r̂ = (# recombinant)/(total).</p></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">LOD Score</div><h3>Testing H₀: r = 0.5</h3><p>Likelihood L(r) = (1−r)^nP · r^nR is binomial in the parental (nP) and recombinant (nR) offspring counts; r̂ = nR/n. LOD(r) = log₁₀[L(r̂)/L(0.5)]. <strong>LOD ≥ 3</strong> is the conventional threshold for declaring significant linkage (1000:1 odds); LOD ≤ −2 is significant evidence against linkage.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-form"><span class="card-icon">🔢</span><div class="card-lbl">LOD ↔ Likelihood Ratio</div><h3>Connecting the Two Frameworks</h3><p>LR = L(r̂)/L(0.5) = 10^Z where Z is the LOD score. The likelihood-ratio chi-square statistic Λ = 2ln(LR) = 2ln(10)·Z ≈ 4.605·Z is approximately χ²(1) under H₀ — so LOD=3 ⇔ Λ≈13.8, far exceeding the χ²(1) critical value of 3.84 at α=0.05.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="eq-block">
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Likelihood (binomial)</span><span class="eq-val">L(r) = (1−r)^nP · r^nR</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">MLE of r</span><span class="eq-val">r̂ = nR / n &nbsp;&nbsp;(n = nP + nR)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">LOD score</span><span class="eq-val">Z(r) = log₁₀[L(r̂)/L(0.5)] &nbsp;&nbsp;significant if Z ≥ 3</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">LR ↔ LOD</span><span class="eq-val">LR = 10^Z &nbsp;&nbsp;; &nbsp;&nbsp;Λ = 2ln(LR) = 4.605·Z ~ χ²(1) under H₀</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G7 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g7">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">07</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Mapping Theory</div><div class="topic-title">Mendel's Laws &amp; <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Mapping Functions</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">⚖️</span><div class="card-lbl">Mendel's Two Laws</div><h3>Segregation &amp; Independent Assortment</h3><ul><li><strong>Segregation:</strong> the two alleles of a gene separate during gamete formation — each gamete gets one allele</li><li><strong>Independent assortment:</strong> alleles of different genes assort independently if on different chromosomes (or far apart) — gives the 9:3:3:1 dihybrid F2 ratio</li><li>Linkage violates independent assortment for genes on the same chromosome</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">📐</span><div class="card-lbl">Why a Map Function?</div><h3>Correcting for Hidden Crossovers</h3><p>A map function m=f(r) converts observed recombination fraction r into additive map distance d. Needed because double crossovers between distant loci restore parental combinations and go undetected — so observed r underestimates d and saturates at 0.5 even as d grows.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">Morgan vs Haldane</div><h3>Two Mapping Functions</h3><ul><li><strong>Morgan:</strong> d = r (no correction) — accurate only for small r (≲0.10)</li><li><strong>Haldane:</strong> r = ½(1−e^(−2d)) ⇔ d = −½ln(1−2r) — assumes Poisson crossovers, no interference; valid over a wider range</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-form"><span class="card-icon">🧮</span><div class="card-lbl">Derivation of Haldane's Function</div><h3>From Poisson Crossovers</h3><p>If N~Poisson(d) crossovers occur in interval AC, a gamete is recombinant iff N is odd. Using P(N even)−P(N odd)=e^(−2d) and P(even)+P(odd)=1 gives P(N odd) = ½(1−e^(−2d)) = r_AC. For small d, r≈d (consistent with 1 cM≈1%); as d→∞, r→0.5.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="eq-block">
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Morgan function</span><span class="eq-val">d = r &nbsp;&nbsp;(valid only for short distances)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Haldane function</span><span class="eq-val">r = ½(1 − e^(−2d)) &nbsp;⇔&nbsp; d = −½ ln(1 − 2r)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Poisson crossover count</span><span class="eq-val">N ~ Poisson(d) &nbsp;&nbsp;; &nbsp;&nbsp;recombinant iff N is odd</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Additivity of map distance</span><span class="eq-val">d_AC = d_AB + d_BC &nbsp;&nbsp;(N_AC = N_AB + N_BC, sum of independent Poissons)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Non-additivity of r</span><span class="eq-val">r_AC = r_AB + r_BC − 2·r_AB·r_BC &nbsp;&nbsp;(NOT simply additive)</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="viz-wrap">
      <div class="viz-title">Recombination Fraction r vs Map Distance d — Morgan vs Haldane</div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 560 220" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="'DM Mono',monospace">
