      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G8 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g8">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">08</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Gene Expression</div><div class="topic-title">Differential Gene Expression &amp; <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Microarray Workflow</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">📊</span><div class="card-lbl">DE Genes</div><h3>What "Differentially Expressed" Means</h3><p>Gene expression = abundance of the RNA transcript (and ultimately protein) in a cell/tissue. A gene is differentially expressed (DE) when its observed change between conditions (disease vs healthy, treated vs untreated) is unlikely to be due to random/technical variation alone.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">⚙️</span><div class="card-lbl">Detection Methods</div><h3>From Fold-Change to GLMs</h3><ul><li>Fold-change ratio with cut-off</li><li>Two-sample / Welch's t-test (gene-by-gene)</li><li>ANOVA for &gt;2 groups</li><li><strong>SAM:</strong> regularised t-statistic + permutation FDR</li><li><strong>Empirical Bayes (limma):</strong> borrows info across genes, stabilises variance for small n</li><li><strong>edgeR / DESeq2:</strong> negative-binomial GLMs for over-dispersed RNA-seq counts</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">SAM's Innovation</div><h3>The "Fudge Factor"</h3><p>SAM adds a small constant s₀ to the SE denominator, stabilising the test statistic for genes with very small variance (preventing spurious significance), and estimates FDR directly via permutation of group labels — more robust than a plain t-test in small-sample microarray settings.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-warn"><span class="card-icon">⚠️</span><div class="card-lbl">Why DE Analysis Matters</div><h3>Importance</h3><ul><li>Identifies candidate genes/pathways for disease or treatment response</li><li>Discovers biomarkers for diagnosis, prognosis, drug targets</li><li>Reveals molecular mechanisms behind phenotypic differences</li><li>Forms the basis of precision medicine</li></ul></div>
    </div>
    <div class="eq-block">
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Two-sample t-test (per gene)</span><span class="eq-val">t = (x̄₁ − x̄₂) / √(s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">SAM-modified statistic</span><span class="eq-val">d = (x̄₁−x̄₂) / (SE + s₀) &nbsp;&nbsp;(s₀ = fudge factor)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">Multiple testing</span><span class="eq-val">Benjamini–Hochberg FDR correction across thousands of genes</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><strong>cDNA Microarray Workflow</strong>RNA extraction → reverse transcription to fluorescently-labelled cDNA → hybridisation onto the array → scanning to quantify signal intensity → normalisation (correcting dye/array bias) → statistical DE testing (t-test/SAM/limma) with FDR correction.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G9 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g9">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">09</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Sequencing Technology</div><div class="topic-title">Genome Sequencing — <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">Sanger vs NGS</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">🧫</span><div class="card-lbl">What it is</div><h3>Determining Base Order</h3><p>Genome sequencing determines the complete, precise order of nucleotide bases (A,T,G,C) across all chromosomes of an organism. Methods include Sanger (chain-termination), Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS), and third-generation long-read sequencing (PacBio, Oxford Nanopore).</p></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">⚖️</span><div class="card-lbl">Sanger vs NGS</div><h3>Key Trade-offs</h3><ul><li><strong>Throughput:</strong> low, one fragment at a time (Sanger) vs millions in parallel (NGS)</li><li><strong>Read length:</strong> long &amp; accurate ~700–1000bp (Sanger) vs short ~100–300bp (short-read NGS)</li><li><strong>Cost/base:</strong> high (Sanger) vs dramatically lower (NGS)</li><li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> very high per-read (Sanger, gold standard) vs lower per-read but high aggregate accuracy via deep coverage (NGS)</li><li><strong>Use:</strong> single-gene confirmation (Sanger) vs whole-genome/exome, RNA-seq, population studies (NGS)</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">WGS, WES &amp; Shotgun Strategy</div><h3>Strategic Choices</h3><p>Whole Genome Sequencing (WGS) covers everything; Whole Exome Sequencing (WES) targets only protein-coding exons — cheaper, focused on coding variants. Shotgun sequencing randomly fragments the genome, sequences overlapping pieces, and computationally reassembles them.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-where"><span class="card-icon">✅</span><div class="card-lbl">Applications</div><h3>Where Sequencing Is Used</h3><ul><li>Disease-gene &amp; cancer-driver mutation discovery</li><li>Personalised/precision medicine</li><li>Pathogen genomics &amp; outbreak surveillance</li><li>Agricultural genomics, forensics, pharmacogenomics</li><li>Comparative &amp; evolutionary genomics</li></ul></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider">· · ·</div>

  <!-- G10 -->
  <div class="topic-section" id="g10">
    <div class="topic-header"><div class="topic-num" style="color:#a8e0cc;">10</div><div class="topic-title-block"><div class="topic-label">Genome-Wide Mapping</div><div class="topic-title">Linkage Analysis, LD vs LE &amp; <span style="color:#0e5c4a;">OMICS Overview</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card c-what"><span class="card-icon">🔬</span><div class="card-lbl">Linkage Analysis</div><h3>Goals in Statistical Genomics</h3><ul><li>Detect &amp; confirm linkage between trait/disease loci and markers</li><li>Construct genetic (linkage) maps — order &amp; distances of loci</li><li>Localise disease genes/QTLs to chromosomal regions</li><li>Estimate recombination fractions and modes of inheritance</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-how"><span class="card-icon">⚖️</span><div class="card-lbl">LD vs LE</div><h3>Linkage Disequilibrium vs Equilibrium</h3><ul><li><strong>LE:</strong> alleles at two loci associate by chance (D≈0) — loci far apart or many generations of recombination</li><li><strong>LD:</strong> non-random allele association (D≠0) — loci physically close, recent mutation, selection, drift, admixture</li><li>LD is the basis of GWAS tag-SNP mapping; strong LD complicates fine-mapping of the causal variant</li></ul></div>
      <div class="card c-idea"><span class="card-icon">💡</span><div class="card-lbl">Huntington's Disease</div><h3>A Landmark Linkage Success</h3><p>Gusella et al. (1983) mapped Huntington's disease to chromosome 4p16.3 using RFLP marker G8 in a Venezuelan family via LOD-score linkage analysis — before the causal gene (HTT) was known. HTT was cloned a decade later (1993), revealing the CAG-repeat expansion. This established the strategy of positional cloning for many Mendelian diseases.</p></div>
      <div class="card c-form"><span class="card-icon">🧬</span><div class="card-lbl">OMICS Family</div><h3>Beyond the Genome</h3><ul><li><strong>Genomics:</strong> the complete DNA/gene set</li><li><strong>Transcriptomics:</strong> the complete RNA transcript set</li><li><strong>Proteomics:</strong> the complete protein set</li><li><strong>Metabolomics:</strong> the complete small-molecule metabolite set</li><li><strong>Epigenomics:</strong> genome-wide chemical/regulatory modifications</li><li><strong>Metagenomics:</strong> genetic material from environmental/microbial communities</li><li><strong>Pharmacogenomics:</strong> genome's influence on drug response</li></ul></div>
    </div>
    <div class="eq-block">
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">LD measures</span><span class="eq-val">D = pᴬᴮ − pᴬ·pᴮ &nbsp;&nbsp;(also expressed as D' or r² for normalised LD)</span></div>
      <div class="eq-row"><span class="eq-name">LE condition</span><span class="eq-val">D ≈ 0 &nbsp;&nbsp;(alleles associate as expected by chance)</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note"><strong>Why Bioinformatics Matters in Health</strong>Disease diagnosis &amp; biomarker discovery, precision medicine, drug discovery &amp; design, genetic counselling, pathogen surveillance, vaccine development, cancer genomics, and large-scale clinical/EHR data mining all rely on the OMICS disciplines above, integrated through statistical genomics methods.</div>
  </div>

